WEBVTT

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:04.280
You know, right now across the whole country,
we are facing this massive systemic

00:00:04.281 --> 00:00:05.880
shortage of healthcare workers.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.920
I mean, we hear about it on
the news all the time, right?

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.560
It affects literally all of us.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.040
But here's the thing most
people completely miss.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.800
We are not going to solve this shortage
at the hospital bedside alone.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.560
Dr. Yolande Nozil,
she's the founder and CEO of GA Healthcare

00:00:19.561 --> 00:00:22.360
Training and Consulting,
actually hit the nail on the head here.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.200
She points out that the real
front lines of this crisis,

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.760
they're in the classrooms,
they're in the skills labs.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.440
The solution literally starts with the
instructors who are preparing that next

00:00:31.441 --> 00:00:33.280
generation of caregivers.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.480
So, welcome in.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.887
I am so excited to have you
here as we unpack all of this.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.560
Today, we're diving into exactly
how this one organization,

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.520
GA Healthcare Training and Consulting,
is tackling this huge challenge head on.

00:00:47.521 --> 00:00:51.520
We're going to look at their exact blueprint,
how they onboard their instructors,

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:55.720
how they set these totally uncompromising
standards, and how they ultimately build

00:00:55.721 --> 00:00:57.687
a resilient healthcare workforce.

00:00:57.727 --> 00:01:01.487
So, whether you're an educator, a
healthcare pro, or just kind of fascinated

00:01:01.488 --> 00:01:04.967
by how systems are built to,
you know, actually save lives,

00:01:05.007 --> 00:01:07.367
you are definitely in the right place.

00:01:07.407 --> 00:01:09.647
Okay, here's our roadmap
for this explainer.

00:01:09.687 --> 00:01:12.487
It's a quick look at how we
build the workforce of tomorrow.

00:01:12.527 --> 00:01:15.647
We'll start with the bedside shortage
solution, move into the four pillars

00:01:15.648 --> 00:01:19.527
of the organization, check out their
core values in action, and wrap up

00:01:19.528 --> 00:01:21.327
with expectations and real impact.

00:01:21.328 --> 00:01:22.407
Let's get to it.

00:01:22.447 --> 00:01:26.407
Let's jump right into section one,
the bedside shortage solution, and look at where

00:01:26.408 --> 00:01:28.727
the real work actually begins.

00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:32.167
Building skills, changing lives,
advancing healthcare.

00:01:32.207 --> 00:01:34.847
Now, that sounds like a great
marketing slogan, doesn't it?

00:01:34.887 --> 00:01:38.967
But for GH Healthcare Training and
Consulting, or GHCTC for short,

00:01:39.007 --> 00:01:41.647
it's actually their absolute
foundational mission.

00:01:41.648 --> 00:01:45.607
They've been based out of Lilburn, Georgia
since 2015, and they realized early

00:01:45.608 --> 00:01:48.447
on that you can't just talk
about advancing healthcare.

00:01:48.487 --> 00:01:52.287
You have to physically build the skills
in the people doing the heavy lifting.

00:01:52.327 --> 00:01:55.284
That single idea drives literally
every decision they make.

00:01:55.324 --> 00:01:58.324
all the way down to how they
orient a brand new instructor.

00:01:58.364 --> 00:02:03.804
Moving right along to section two,
the four pillars of GAD CHTC.

00:02:04.164 --> 00:02:07.084
This is our organizational foundation.

00:02:07.124 --> 00:02:10.364
To see how they actually build these
skills, we have to look at what

00:02:10.365 --> 00:02:11.764
they do day-to-day.

00:02:11.804 --> 00:02:16.444
GHCTC operates across four really
diverse but totally connected areas.

00:02:16.484 --> 00:02:19.124
First up, certification and continuing ed.

00:02:19.164 --> 00:02:21.804
They're an authorized American
Heart Association training center.

00:02:21.844 --> 00:02:24.604
So think basic life support and pals.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:28.844
Second is workforce and career entry training,
which is absolutely crucial for roles

00:02:28.845 --> 00:02:30.284
like medication aides.

00:02:30.324 --> 00:02:34.764
Third, they offer nursing education
support like really intense NCLEX reviews.

00:02:34.804 --> 00:02:39.124
And finally, consulting services for
legal, regulatory and leadership guidance.

00:02:39.164 --> 00:02:42.764
So they are operating at every single
level of the healthcare pipeline.

00:02:42.804 --> 00:02:47.684
And because they operate at every level,
their classrooms are incredibly diverse.

00:02:47.724 --> 00:02:48.884
I mean, picture this.

00:02:48.924 --> 00:02:53.444
An instructor could be looking at the novice,
an 18-year-old who has literally never

00:02:53.445 --> 00:02:55.187
even worn scrubs before.

00:02:55.227 --> 00:02:58.627
Sitting right next to them, the veteran,
a seasoned nurse who is renewing

00:02:58.628 --> 00:03:00.947
a certification for like the 10th time.

00:03:00.987 --> 00:03:05.267
And then right behind them is the career changer,
someone taking a massive financial

00:03:05.268 --> 00:03:09.027
risk on a 4-week course just
trying to find a better path.

00:03:09.067 --> 00:03:11.347
The message to instructors
is crystal clear.

00:03:11.387 --> 00:03:14.827
No matter who is sitting in that chair,
every single learner gets the exact

00:03:14.828 --> 00:03:17.507
same uncompromising
standard of instruction.

00:03:17.547 --> 00:03:21.387
Which brings us to section 3,
core values in action.

00:03:21.427 --> 00:03:25.947
Because actually delivering on those high
standards takes a lot more than just words

00:03:25.948 --> 00:03:27.187
on a page.

00:03:27.227 --> 00:03:30.787
Gay HCT takes their core
values incredibly literally.

00:03:30.827 --> 00:03:32.507
Take compassion, for example.

00:03:32.547 --> 00:03:36.947
Look, in education, it's super easy to just
get bogged down in the curriculum, right?

00:03:36.987 --> 00:03:41.107
But instructors here are taught to see
the actual human being in front of them.

00:03:41.147 --> 00:03:44.347
These learners are showing
up with heavy, full lives.

00:03:44.387 --> 00:03:48.827
They bring job stress, childcare issues,
transportation problems, and honestly,

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:51.027
a lot of real fear into the classroom.

00:03:51.067 --> 00:03:53.601
So compassion means teaching
that actual person.

00:03:53.641 --> 00:03:56.761
Not just rushing through a learning
objective on a lesson plan.

00:03:56.801 --> 00:04:00.321
Now, you might be wondering, does that
compassion mean they lower the bar,

00:04:00.361 --> 00:04:02.601
like cut them some slack on the grades?

00:04:02.641 --> 00:04:07.281
Absolutely not. Excellence is another
core value and they hold the line.

00:04:07.321 --> 00:04:11.361
For any written assessment,
the passing threshold is a strict 80%.

00:04:11.401 --> 00:04:14.001
No grading on a curve,
no lowering the bar.

00:04:14.041 --> 00:04:18.401
If you're going into healthcare,
you simply have to know the material inside and out.

00:04:18.441 --> 00:04:21.674
But wait, it actually gets even stricter.

00:04:21.801 --> 00:04:26.561
When we talk about skills, the physical
tasks you actually perform as a caregiver,

00:04:26.601 --> 00:04:30.721
that passing threshold
jumps from 80% to 100%.

00:04:31.001 --> 00:04:34.434
Complete, absolute competency,
nothing less.

00:04:34.521 --> 00:04:36.441
And that's a huge leap, right?

00:04:36.481 --> 00:04:39.641
Why demand total perfection
on a skill check?

00:04:39.681 --> 00:04:40.961
Well, here is why.

00:04:41.001 --> 00:04:45.561
Because a skill performed at 90% on
a real living patient is a skill

00:04:45.562 --> 00:04:47.662
performed wrong, period.

00:04:47.841 --> 00:04:52.681
GHCT makes it so clear to their instructors,
holding this super strict line isn't

00:04:52.682 --> 00:04:53.498
being mean.

00:04:53.538 --> 00:04:57.178
Actually, it is the most respectful,
compassionate thing you can do for a student

00:04:57.218 --> 00:05:00.178
who is one day going to be alone
in a room with someone's mother.

00:05:00.218 --> 00:05:03.578
When you frame it like that,
when the stakes are quite literally life,

00:05:03.579 --> 00:05:09.058
dignity, and care, 100% competency is
genuinely the only acceptable outcome.

00:05:09.098 --> 00:05:13.298
And that leads right into their
third core value, integrity.

00:05:13.338 --> 00:05:16.578
In this field, paperwork isn't
just annoying bureaucracy.

00:05:16.618 --> 00:05:19.498
It's a literal, legal,
and ethical lifeline.

00:05:19.538 --> 00:05:23.818
Instructors have to document exactly what
happened, follow every single regulation

00:05:23.819 --> 00:05:27.978
to the letter, and do the right thing
when absolutely nobody is watching.

00:05:28.018 --> 00:05:30.938
The entire organization's reputation,
their state approvals,

00:05:30.978 --> 00:05:34.898
their clinical partnerships, the whole
shebang rests entirely on the honest

00:05:34.899 --> 00:05:36.778
documentation of its instructors.

00:05:36.818 --> 00:05:40.058
Integrity is basically the
glue holding it all together.

00:05:40.098 --> 00:05:45.218
Let's head into our final section,
section four, expectations in real impact,

00:05:45.258 --> 00:05:48.098
focusing on the actual
role of the instructor.

00:05:48.138 --> 00:05:50.386
This whole system is a
true two-way street.

00:05:50.426 --> 00:05:54.226
The organization doesn't just hand an
instructor a roster and say, "Hey,

00:05:54.266 --> 00:05:55.186
good luck out there."

00:05:55.226 --> 00:05:57.466
Instructors can expect full backing.

00:05:57.506 --> 00:06:01.226
We're talking full curriculum,
access to the learning management system,

00:06:01.266 --> 00:06:04.626
and really importantly,
early access to leadership.

00:06:04.666 --> 00:06:08.026
Dr. Noziel wants to know if
something isn't working right away.

00:06:08.066 --> 00:06:10.586
But in return, the expectations are heavy.

00:06:10.626 --> 00:06:13.986
Instructors need to be prepared
before anyone even walks in.

00:06:14.026 --> 00:06:17.586
They need to be present, be perfectly
accurate with their evaluations,

00:06:17.626 --> 00:06:21.306
and be highly professional because
when they are out at a clinical site,

00:06:21.346 --> 00:06:25.226
to that facility,
that instructor literally is GHSDCT.

00:06:25.266 --> 00:06:29.906
When both sides meet those expectations,
you get this really beautiful sequential

00:06:29.907 --> 00:06:31.306
ladder of success.

00:06:31.346 --> 00:06:35.866
It starts at step one, instructional consistency,
meaning everyone gets the same top

00:06:35.867 --> 00:06:40.186
tier quality. That naturally feeds
into step two, regulatory integrity,

00:06:40.226 --> 00:06:42.066
keeping everything fully compliant.

00:06:42.106 --> 00:06:46.986
Those two things combined trigger step
three, learner success, high pass rates.

00:06:47.026 --> 00:06:50.358
And a great environment leads to
step four, instructor retention.

00:06:50.398 --> 00:06:56.078
People actually want to stay, which all culminates
in step five, organizational reputation.

00:06:56.118 --> 00:06:59.998
It is just a highly logical,
incredibly effective chain reaction.

00:07:00.038 --> 00:07:04.318
But you know, strip away all the
regulations, the rubrics, the software,

00:07:04.358 --> 00:07:06.918
and it all boils down to one simple truth.

00:07:06.958 --> 00:07:09.038
The real job is the student.

00:07:09.078 --> 00:07:11.238
The curriculum, that's just the tool.

00:07:11.278 --> 00:07:14.598
During orientation, instructors are
given this scenario to think about.

00:07:14.638 --> 00:07:16.998
Imagine you're teaching
your very first cohort.

00:07:17.038 --> 00:07:20.838
Somewhere in that room is a student who
deep down really doesn't believe they

00:07:20.839 --> 00:07:24.478
can succeed and at some point
they're going to fail a skill check.

00:07:24.518 --> 00:07:28.438
In that exact moment of failure, that
student is going to strongly consider just

00:07:28.439 --> 00:07:29.958
quitting right then and there.

00:07:29.998 --> 00:07:32.238
So will that struggling student stay?

00:07:32.278 --> 00:07:33.998
That is the ultimate question here.

00:07:34.038 --> 00:07:37.518
Because whether they dig deep and push
through or just pack up their bag

00:07:37.519 --> 00:07:41.678
and leave forever comes down almost
entirely to how that instructor handles

00:07:41.679 --> 00:07:43.518
that exact moment of failure.

00:07:43.558 --> 00:07:47.084
The competent, confident healthcare
worker we desperately need at the bedside

00:07:47.124 --> 00:07:49.923
Their future is literally in the
hands of the person at the front

00:07:49.924 --> 00:07:51.003
of the classroom.

00:07:51.043 --> 00:07:54.643
It really makes you realize just how
incredibly powerful a prepared educator

00:07:54.644 --> 00:07:56.123
actually is, doesn't it?

00:07:56.163 --> 00:07:58.283
Thank you so much for
exploring this with me today.

00:07:58.323 --> 00:08:01.490
Keep asking questions and keep learning.